    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "Both the targeted acceptance test and the full test suite are green (`BUILD SUCCESS`).\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec required all rejection responses (400, 409, 429) to return an RFC7807 `application/problem+json` body with `type`, `title`, `status`, and `detail`.\n\n**What I found:** Rejections all flow through OfficeFloor escalation handlers in `.../rest/escalation/`. The 409 (`DuplicateIdentity`, `CityAtCapacity`), 429 (`DailyOwnerLimit`), and the `MethodArgumentNotValid` 400 handlers already emitted a Spring `ProblemDetail` via the shared `ProblemDetails.build(...)` helper (which sets `type=about:blank`, `title=<exception simple name>`, `status`, `detail`). Serializing a `ProblemDetail` through the Spring MVC bridge yields the `application/problem+json` content type automatically.\n\n**The gap:** Five 400 handlers still returned a plain `Map.of(\"errors\", ...)` body (content type `application/json`, no RFC7807 members):\n- `InvalidEmailExceptionHandler`\n- `InvalidPostcodeExceptionHandler`\n- `InvalidTelephoneExceptionHandler`\n- `MissingOwnerFieldsExceptionHandler`\n- `FutureRegistrationDateExceptionHandler`\n\n**The fix:** Converted each to build a `ProblemDetail` via `ProblemDetails.build(ex, HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST, <detail>)` and send it as `ResponseEntity<ProblemDetail>`, matching the existing 409/429 handlers. I preserved the original field information as an `errors` extension member (RFC7807 permits extension members), so no information is lost while the four required members are now always present. HTTP status codes are unchanged.\n\nThe new `AcceptanceTest.coreRejectionIsProblemJson` (POST `/api/owners` with a missing `city` \u2192 `MissingOwnerFieldsException`) now asserts `application/problem+json` and `$.status == 400`, and passes.",
